30. Quick Revision Cheat Sheet

WINDOW FUNCTION
→ Calculation across related rows without collapsing them

OVER()
→ Defines the window

PARTITION BY
→ Divides rows into groups

ORDER BY
→ Defines calculation order

ROW_NUMBER()
→ Unique sequential number

RANK()
→ Ties + gaps

DENSE_RANK()
→ Ties + no gaps

LAG()
→ Previous row

LEAD()
→ Next row

SUM() OVER()
→ Running / partitioned total

AVG() OVER()
→ Running / partitioned average

MIN() OVER()
→ Window minimum

MAX() OVER()
→ Window maximum

COUNT() OVER()
→ Window count

31. Most Important Patterns to Memorize

Ranking

ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
    ORDER BY salary DESC
)

Ranking per group

ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
    PARTITION BY department_id
    ORDER BY salary DESC
)

Top-N per group

WITH ranked AS (
    SELECT
        *,
        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
            PARTITION BY department_id
            ORDER BY salary DESC
        ) AS rn
    FROM employees
)
SELECT *
FROM ranked
WHERE rn <= 3;

Previous row

LAG(value) OVER (
    ORDER BY date
)

Next row

LEAD(value) OVER (
    ORDER BY date
)

Running total

SUM(amount) OVER (
    ORDER BY date
    ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW
)

Running total per group

SUM(amount) OVER (
    PARTITION BY customer_id
    ORDER BY date
)

Latest record per customer

WITH ranked AS (
    SELECT
        *,
        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
            PARTITION BY customer_id
            ORDER BY updated_at DESC
        ) AS rn
    FROM customers
)
SELECT *
FROM ranked
WHERE rn = 1;

32. Data Engineering Connection

A realistic SQL transformation can look like:

Source System
     ↓
Raw Data
     ↓
Data Cleaning
     ↓
Deduplication
     ↓
ROW_NUMBER()
     ↓
Keep Latest Records
     ↓
LAG / LEAD
     ↓
Detect Changes
     ↓
Aggregations
     ↓
Running Metrics
     ↓
Business Transformations
     ↓
Data Warehouse

Window Functions are therefore an important part of SQL-based ETL/ELT transformations.

